WEBVTT

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:07.900
<i>Now it comes down to Miyagawa-kun's 
volley and my practiced footwork!</i>

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:10.190
<i>Here it comes!</i>

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.410
<i>A d-drive volley?!</i>

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:26.120
<i>This is my new weapon that's 
going to help me change!</i>

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.880
Incredible! He pulled off a full swing on a volley!

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.850
How did that even go in?

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:02.800
<i>I see...</i>

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.640
<i>For a drive volley, you run up, 
but don't change your form.</i>

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.540
<i>He can do a full swing like 
he does with his stroke.</i>

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.360
<i>As expected, Miyagawa-kun really is strong!</i>

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.360
The Speed of Evolution

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.270
<i>A drive volley is indeed a threat,</i>

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:24.320
<i>but I'm glad I got to see it early.</i>

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:25.700
<i>It's a one-set match,</i>

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.560
<i>so I want the data on all
threats as early as possible.</i>

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.030
<i>And he got a point off me only
because it was the second serve.</i>

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:34.590
<i>As long as I get the first serve in...</i>

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.590
<i>I'll put a slight spin on this...</i>

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:40.130
<i>and increase my chances of 
getting my first serve in!</i>

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:42.170
<i>Good!</i>

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:43.420
<i>I can't move up.</i>

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.830
<i>I'll hit it back down the line 
using my 1/64 control...</i>

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:48.610
<i>And win it with the next shot!</i>

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:51.150
40-15.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:52.190
<i>Yes!</i>

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:53.570
<i>I can pull ahead on my serve.</i>

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:55.150
<i>I won't let him break!</i>

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:56.530
He did it!

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:57.730
Everyone, everyone...

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.410
Oh, Yukichi.

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:01.140
I won my second-round match!

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.450
I'm going to the Kanto Junior!

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.150
Wow, good job.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:06.640
Congrats!

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.130
I was against a lower rank,

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:09.970
so it makes sense that I won, though.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:11.350
So how's Bro doing?

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:12.920
Take a look.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:15.680
All right!

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.530
Game: Maruo. 1-0.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:24.350
<i>Single-handed strokes and drive volleys...</i>

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:27.140
<i>Miyagawa-kun certainly does 
have a powerful weapon.</i>

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.780
<i>But my serve is working, too!</i>

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.970
<i>As long as my first serve goes in, 
I can hold serve in this match.</i>

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:36.050
<i>And if I can hold serve,</i>

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.190
<i>I can afford to take risks 
during my return game!</i>

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.810
<i>I should've known he wouldn't let 
me break his serve so easily.</i>

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:45.200
<i>But I was able to score with my drive volley.</i>

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:47.700
<i>The rest is up to how I use it.</i>

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:48.950
Time!

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:51.340
<i>I'm sure his serve has evolved as well,</i>

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:55.210
<i>but for now, I have to rely on previous 
data, watch carefully, and judge...</i>

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:56.210
<i>So fast!</i>

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.050
<i>He moved up from that strong shot!</i>

00:03:59.050 --> 00:03:59.710
<i>Can I get past him?!</i>

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:02.800
15-love.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.220
<i>Drive volley...</i>

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.720
<i>I have to do something 
about that drive volley.</i>

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:10.880
<i>First and foremost, don't let him move up.</i>

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:15.230
<i>That means I can't give him a chance 
to move up after his ground stroke!</i>

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:17.950
<i>Which means...</i>

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:20.690
<i>I can't be flustered by his powerful stroke!</i>

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.740
Side Spin          Top Spin          Slice

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.740
Cuts left or right    Sinks then bounces    Doesn't bounce

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:23.740
<i>Determine what shot it'll be early on...</i>

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:27.240
Top Spin          Slice          Side Stroke

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.650
<i>Control it well, and don't let him move up!</i>

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:32.390
<i>But relying on my stroke 
will just get me cornered.</i>

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.460
<i>A strong, flat hit down the line!</i>

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:38.230
<i>If I hit this haphazardly, it'll give 
him an opportunity to move up!</i>

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:41.930
<i>I'll have to risk it and use my 1/81 control!</i>

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:43.760
<i>It went in!</i>

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.160
<i>But it's not fast enough.</i>

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.260
<i>Miyagawa-kun will be able 
to reach it with one hand!</i>

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:49.760
30-love.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:53.210
<i>I didn't even let him move up, and yet...</i>

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:57.030
<i>I should've known using strokes to hold a 
demon of strokes at bay would be tough.</i>

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:58.650
<i>If that's the case,</i>

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:02.280
<i>I have to turn it around during my return, 
before it becomes a ground stroke battle!</i>

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:05.780
40-love.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:07.680
<i>Wide with a slice!</i>

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.610
<i>It's tough when he gets his first serve to go in.</i>

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.350
<i>But compared to a year ago,</i>

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:15.290
<i>his serve's evolved the least.</i>

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.290
He's running up after the serve!

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:20.790
<i>He's pushing forward!</i>

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:23.900
<i>Prevent him from getting a strong 
hit by aiming at his feet...</i>

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:26.000
<i>But a shot like that will get returned.</i>

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:29.640
<i>Swinging for a drive volley takes preparation.</i>

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.390
<i>So I have to go faster!</i>

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:38.570
Game: Miyagawa. 1-all.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.020
<i>I aimed too much.</i>

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:41.900
<i>But...</i>

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:45.620
<i>Miyagawa-kun wasn't fully 
prepared for the swing that time.</i>

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:49.160
<i>If I'd gotten the ball in, it would've been 
a regular volley instead of a drive volley.</i>

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:50.900
<i>Even though the risk is high,</i>

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:53.910
<i>I have a chance if I can hit 
a hard shot near his feet.</i>

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:55.750
<i>And although it's crude,</i>

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:58.750
<i>I've decided to chip away at 
him, starting with serves.</i>

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:01.290
<i>In a one-set match, 
victory goes to the swiftest.</i>

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:04.420
<i>Whoever breaks first will have 
an overwhelming advantage.</i>

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:07.420
<i>I have to hold serve so I can 
move on to breaking his serve.</i>

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:10.550
<i>To do that, I have to get my first serve in.</i>

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:13.110
<i>I'll keep my chance of success over 70% with</i>

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:14.430
<i>the fastest serve I can hit!</i>

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:19.500
<i>After it goes in, I'll use my advantage to 
keep him from going for the drive volley.</i>

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.520
<i>I won't ease up on my offense 
and won't let him move up!</i>

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:25.120
<i>His strokes are just as good one-handed.</i>

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.820
<i>Be aware of his wider range of 
coverage and use my control!</i>

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:30.590
15-love.

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:31.280
Yes!

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:37.290
<i>While that flat serve is still 
ingrained in Miyagawa-kun's brain...</i>

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:39.620
<i>Go for a slice!</i>

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:41.700
<i>Use the two-phase method I learned in Florida,</i>

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.170
<i>and score with it!</i>

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:44.900
Yes!

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:45.920
30-love.

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:48.280
<i>His serving speed and stability.</i>

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:50.600
<i>His stroke strength and its accuracy.</i>

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.410
<i>His decisiveness in volleys.</i>

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.930
<i>His offensive style of play, 
along with his footwork...</i>

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:58.680
<i>The improvement Maruo-kun has 
shown this past year is extraordinary.</i>

00:06:58.940 --> 00:06:59.880
<i>Calm down.</i>

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.450
<i>It's only natural to be at a
disadvantage in a return game.</i>

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:08.190
<i>And it's not as if his serves are impossible to hit.</i>

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:12.040
40-love.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:13.700
<i>They're getting faster?!</i>

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.960
Game: Maruo. 2-1.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.580
That guy's doing really well 
against a third-seeded player.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.580
Maruo

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:23.940
<i>This is beyond my expectations.</i>

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.480
<i>That serve can't be returned 
on technique alone.</i>

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.800
<i>The basis for technique, 
his body itself, has evolved.</i>

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:35.350
<i>His explosive force and flexibility are 
aiding the evolution of all his techniques.</i>

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:39.050
<i>I should consider him a different 
person from who he was last year.</i>

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:46.040
<i>In the time it took for me to master 
single-handed strokes and drive volleys...</i>

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:50.510
<i>How did he evolve so much more 
during the same time period?!</i>

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:52.890
He's already drinking kobu tea.

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:54.630
He's drinking tea during a match?

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:56.620
Who cares when he drinks tea?

00:07:56.620 --> 00:08:00.870
No, he drinks kobu tea only when 
he's either trying to improve his focus

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:03.120
or switch gears.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:06.070
The score is only 2-1, 
and they're both holding serve,

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:08.130
so it just seems early for that.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.780
I personally think it's just the right time.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:11.600
What?!

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:13.640
So you think they're at a pivotal point already?

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:14.130
Time!

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:21.590
<i>Maruo-kun is incredible for</i>

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:24.730
<i>evolving his entire tennis style 
in such a short period of time.</i>

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:27.670
<i>But evolving isn't just about the 
number of new things learned.</i>

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:31.970
<i>I've only evolved in my 
drive volleys and strokes.</i>

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:34.830
<i>But since I concentrated all my 
effort into those techniques,</i>

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:36.160
<i>the effect is increased!</i>

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:39.160
<i>What I learned from the last game...</i>

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:42.860
<i>It's tough going toe to toe with 
ground strokes during my return game.</i>

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:52.190
| Serve |
More power but no change

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:47.190
<i>So I should aim for a winner 
when I return his serve.</i>

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:49.770
<i>Miyagawa-kun's serves are more powerful,</i>

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:51.970
<i>but there aren't any other 
significant changes.</i>

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:58.680
Wide     Front     Center     Front     Wide

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:56.220
<i>Which means it's likely that my data 
from last year will be helpful.</i>

00:08:56.220 --> 00:09:01.180
<i>If that's true, then it's more than 
60% likely that it'll be wide!</i>

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:01.680
<i>Here it comes!</i>

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:03.930
Love-15.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:05.540
What an amazing return!

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:05.690
All right!

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:06.600
He went straight for it!

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:07.690
<i>He read my serve!</i>

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:08.490
<i>Then...</i>

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:09.940
<i>Forward spin!</i>

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:12.190
<i>He's going off-center!</i>

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.450
<i>Damn... It's going over the net!</i>

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:16.950
<i>But don't panic.</i>

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:19.370
<i>If I can hit a fast ball near his feet,</i>

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:20.870
<i>he can't use his drive volley!</i>

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:27.200
Ei-chan's moved up, too!

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:30.760
Love-30.

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:31.710
Yes!

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:34.830
Why didn't Miyagawa use his drive volley?

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:38.640
You can't swing when a ball 
that fast comes at your feet.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.490
He kept him from hitting a drive volley,

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:43.870
then anticipated that the ball would 
pop up and went up for the shot.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:53.630
Miyagawa

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:47.160
There's not enough space to hit it 
past Miyagawa from the sides.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:50.780
Shooting a fast ball near his feet, 
since it's closer with more space,

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:53.970
is less risky, so long as he doesn't hit the net.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:57.650
It's a great method for getting 
around a drive volley.

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:00.550
<i>He went after my drive volley.</i>

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.470
<i>Drive volleys aren't strong against fast balls.</i>

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:05.790
<i>I shouldn't have moved up aggressively like that.</i>

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:08.170
<i>I have to corner him with my ground stroke first.</i>

00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:12.410
<i>To do that, I need to gain the 
upper hand with my serve.</i>

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:15.420
<i>But even my serve is a target now.</i>

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:17.600
<i>First, I have to fluster his serve.</i>

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:19.210
<i>On my return, I have to take the initiative</i>

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:20.580
<i>and prevent him from moving forward.</i>

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:22.150
<i>If he moves forward anyway,</i>

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:25.430
<i>hit a fast ball at his feet, making 
him unable to use his drive volley.</i>

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:28.680
<i>I now know exactly what 
to do to break his serve!</i>

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:30.780
<i>Love-30...</i>

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:43.680
Wide     Front     Center    Front     Wide

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:34.290
<i>Right now, Miyagawa-kun will definitely 
want to get his first serve in.</i>

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:38.390
<i>So it's highly likely that he'll 
go wide, his strong suit.</i>

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:44.180
<i>But I'm sure he already knows how I plan 
to return that, so he might change it up.</i>

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:47.840
<i>30% of my attention will 
be focused on the center,</i>

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:48.910
<i>and the rest on wide.</i>

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:50.170
<i>It's wide!</i>

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:51.270
Out!

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:52.690
15-30.

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:54.030
<i>Damn!</i>

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:56.790
<i>I should've focused 100% on wide!</i>

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:59.420
<i>I was leading in a return game...</i>

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:01.350
<i>This is no time to be careful!</i>

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:02.680
<i>I have to break his serve!</i>

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:06.180
<i>So far it's been wide, center, then wide.</i>

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.690
<i>He'll most likely go for the center next.</i>

00:11:08.690 --> 00:11:11.350
<i>I'll put 100% of my focus 
on the center this time!</i>

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:12.270
<i>Center!</i>

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:15.790
<i>Not only did he read my serve, 
he moved up to the net!</i>

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:16.780
<i>Single-handed!</i>

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:17.860
<i>But it'll go in!</i>

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:20.070
<i>Reach!</i>

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:29.640
15-40.

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.570
He nailed it on the return dash!

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:32.870
That means...

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:34.660
Maruo's got break point!

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:36.170
That's incredible!

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:36.550
Yes! You can do it, Ei-chan!

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:37.600
You're kidding me, right?

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:39.470
Is Miyagawa not feeling well or something?

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:42.050
No matter how you look at it, 
Maruo's scoring offensively.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:44.410
<i>He'll go for the center next.</i>

00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:45.970
<i>I'm going all-in on this one!</i>

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:47.970
<i>It's wide!</i>

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:50.730
<i>Send it back far and deep, 
then come back quickly!</i>

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:53.700
<i>If Miyagawa-kun doesn't come forward,</i>

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:55.480
<i>it'll turn into a ground stroke battle!</i>

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:59.630
As expected, Miyagawa has 
an edge on ground strokes.

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:00.740
Ei-chan, hang in there!

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:02.240
<i>It's going in!</i>

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:06.360
And how is Miyagawa hitting 
those tight shots on break point?

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:08.410
Isn't he afraid of making an error?

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:12.750
<i>That's just how dire the situation is for him.</i>

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:15.660
<i>I won't get anywhere by playing defensively.</i>

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:20.020
<i>To pull ahead, I have to go for the 
turn-around, even if it's aggressive.</i>

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:21.760
<i>Then...</i>

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:23.970
<i>Which of the various shots should I use?</i>

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:30.640
Flat

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:26.920
<i>Probably flat, since it has the most power.</i>

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:29.000
<i>I'll use my strong delivery, 
be prepared to miss,</i>

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:30.640
<i>and hit it straight into open court!</i>

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:35.450
<i>I couldn't avoid a battle of ground strokes 
with Miyagawa-kun after all...</i>

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:36.650
<i>I just have to face him!</i>

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:39.480
<i>What is this perseverance?!</i>

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:43.900
<i>I can't be beaten on my serve in a battle 
of the ground strokes I'm best at!</i>

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:46.620
<i>It's flat!</i>

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:48.140
<i>Hit it with all my strength!</i>

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:50.260
Out!

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:53.500
<i>I broke his serve!</i>

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:56.370
Game: Maruo. 3-1.

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:58.360
He broke Miyagawa's serve!

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:00.500
Maruo pushed for it, too.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:03.410
I wonder if Miyagawa panicked 
since his drive volleys

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:06.440
were blocked and his strokes 
were returned persistently.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:10.510
Rather than being persistent, Maruo most 
likely planned ahead for the next moves.

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:15.110
The fear of getting scored on 
while playing to his strongest suit

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:17.350
is probably what made Miyagawa miss.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:19.480
<i>I did it!</i>

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:23.410
<i>I was able to get the upper hand by 
breaking serve first in a one-set match!</i>

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:25.350
<i>But my opponent is Miyagawa-kun.</i>

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:27.520
<i>I can't relax after one break.</i>

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:30.790
<i>I'll go for a first serve with 
a 70% success rate again.</i>

00:13:31.210 --> 00:13:34.950
<i>But I'll go flat through the center 
without giving up any speed.</i>

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:36.620
<i>No matter what, I have to hold serve!</i>

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:39.920
<i>Damn...</i>

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:42.860
<i>There's still a 30% risk of failure, 
so I'll switch gears.</i>

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:46.120
<i>For the second serve, 
I'll use a spin shot that's 100%,</i>

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:47.380
<i>and follow the course!</i>

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:51.140
15-love.

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:54.460
<i>He missed the return on the second serve.</i>

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:56.640
<i>I've never seen him make 
such a careless error.</i>

00:13:57.760 --> 00:13:58.600
<i>What is this?</i>

00:13:58.600 --> 00:13:59.590
30-love.

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:01.720
<i>Miyagawa-kun's responses are poor.</i>

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:03.720
<i>Is he flustered by the break?</i>

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:05.480
<i>Is his condition off?</i>

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:07.030
<i>I don't know what's going on,</i>

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:09.560
<i>but I have to keep the lead in this game!</i>

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:15.190
Miyagawa's definitely shaken up by that break.

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:17.640
What? It's only 3-1.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:19.420
It's way too soon to lose hope.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:21.320
<i>He's strong...</i>

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:24.920
<i>At this point, Maruo-kun 
is clearly better than me.</i>

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:28.570
<i>The moment I thought that, 
my body stopped cooperating.</i>

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:31.000
<i>I've never had this happen before.</i>

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.370
<i>No matter how powerful the opponent was,</i>

00:14:33.370 --> 00:14:35.430
<i>I gave it my all and fought 'til the end.</i>

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:38.050
<i>Because it'll carry on to the next game.</i>

00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:39.690
Out. 40-love.

00:14:39.950 --> 00:14:41.410
<i>Next?</i>

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:44.210
<i>Oh, right. There is no "next"...</i>

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:47.820
<i>There's no way a high schooler 
who loses in the prefecturals</i>

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:49.450
<i>can play tennis professionally.</i>

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:52.480
<i>If that's true,</i>

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:55.540
<i>I can't keep up the confidence to go pro.</i>

00:14:56.060 --> 00:14:57.690
Game: Maruo. 4-1.

00:14:57.690 --> 00:14:58.890
Yes!

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:02.410
<i>Is that what I'm afraid of?</i>

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:03.710
Bro!

00:15:03.710 --> 00:15:05.160
Nice hold!

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:07.960
If he continues to hold serve, he'll win, right?

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:11.080
Seriously? Miyagawa's gonna lose here?

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:14.150
Hey, hey, this is getting crazy!

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:21.600
Hey, Miyagawa!

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:31.350
You can do it, Miyagawa!

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:36.240
If you don't do your best here, 
you'll regret it forever!

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:43.560
<i>Oobayashi-senpai...</i>

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:50.580
<i>Regret... forever...</i>

00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:52.420
<i>That's right.</i>

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:53.990
<i>There is a next time.</i>

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:58.530
<i>I might continue tennis in college 
like Oobayashi-senpai did.</i>

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:03.380
<i>Even if it's not tennis, so long as I'm alive,</i>

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:05.730
<i>there'll always be more battles to face.</i>

00:16:06.350 --> 00:16:08.190
<i>But if I give up here and now,</i>

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:11.480
<i>I'll give up next time and 
the next time after that, too.</i>

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:20.330
<i>I don't want that!</i>

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:22.440
That was unexpected.

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:24.040
This guy's really passionate.

00:16:24.310 --> 00:16:27.040
Is Oobayashi-kun like that at school?

00:16:28.580 --> 00:16:29.250
Time!

00:16:32.570 --> 00:16:35.050
Maruo

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:36.610
<i>That's right...</i>

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:40.480
<i>I can't give up yet!</i>

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:48.220
Yes!

00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:49.230
15-love.

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:50.770
<i>That was fast!</i>

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:52.760
<i>That's his fastest ball today.</i>

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:54.820
<i>It's different from the previous games.</i>

00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:57.300
<i>If I can't depend on Miyagawa-kun to miss,</i>

00:16:57.300 --> 00:16:59.730
<i>I'll have to chip away at him 
during his serves like before,</i>

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:01.260
<i>and break again!</i>

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:04.800
<i>He'll definitely get his first serve in.</i>

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:08.630
<i>Which means he's likely to 
use his favored wide serve.</i>

00:17:09.340 --> 00:17:10.090
<i>It's deep!</i>

00:17:12.090 --> 00:17:15.670
<i>He's hitting a risky return with 
an even riskier power shot!</i>

00:17:15.670 --> 00:17:18.900
<i>In this type of game, I still have 
the upper hand, having broken serve!</i>

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:20.490
<i>I'll just go for it!</i>

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:22.060
<i>1/81!</i>

00:17:23.850 --> 00:17:24.850
<i>He's moving up!</i>

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:26.850
<i>A lob!</i>

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:31.110
Out! 15-all.

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:32.600
So close!

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:35.390
Miyagawa changed his pace all of the sudden.

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:39.240
Yeah, Maruo completely missed 
the timing on that one.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:41.390
<i>I'm well aware of our disparity in skill.</i>

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:43.190
<i>But I won't lose!</i>

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:47.080
<i>The same way Maruo-kun beat me last year,</i>

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:49.120
<i>I'll keep pressing, even if it's risky!</i>

00:17:49.540 --> 00:17:53.000
<i>I'll bet all sixteen years of my life on this!</i>

00:17:54.320 --> 00:17:55.590
<i>It's faster again!</i>

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:00.870
30-15.

00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:02.850
<i>The ball's movements are 
completely different!</i>

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:07.090
<i>Even though his play is high-risk,</i>

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:08.230
<i>he's not missing.</i>

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:09.900
<i>This feeling...</i>

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:13.110
<i>It feels like he's relying on his instincts 
and coming at me with all he's got.</i>

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:16.050
<i>He's like me when I played against Alex!</i>

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:22.090
Yes!

00:18:22.090 --> 00:18:23.370
40-15.

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:26.620
Even though drop shots aren't Miyagawa's 
forte, he pulled it off perfectly...

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:28.380
Yeah!

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:30.230
Game: Miyagawa. 4-2.

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:31.130
Maruo leads.

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:33.570
<i>Miyagawa-kun got his rhythm back.</i>

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:35.040
<i>No, it's more than that.</i>

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:36.950
<i>He might be playing his best.</i>

00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:39.880
<i>Even so, I have to hold serve!</i>

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:44.410
<i>The power of the serve, the type of hit, 
and the direction all keep changing.</i>

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:46.390
<i>There are too many patterns.
I can't read them all.</i>

00:18:46.390 --> 00:18:47.640
<i>Even if I can't return it perfectly,</i>

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:49.890
<i>I have my ground strokes!</i>

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:53.430
<i>He's way too aggressive.</i>

00:18:53.430 --> 00:18:55.850
<i>This type of play is bound 
to miss at some point...</i>

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:57.400
<i>And yet they're all going in!</i>

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:01.120
<i>Anything can happen in a match like this!</i>

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:02.770
<i>If I don't crush him, he'll crush me.</i>

00:19:02.770 --> 00:19:04.660
<i>If I get crushed here...</i>

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:05.660
<i>It's over!</i>

00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:07.660
Whoa, what an amazing rally!

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:10.570
All right!

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:11.770
<i>I was able to hold serve!</i>

00:19:11.770 --> 00:19:13.570
Game: Maruo. 5-2.

00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:14.420
Maruo leads.

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:17.910
One more game and Maruo's 
gonna beat out the third seed!

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.180
There's only been one break. You never know!

00:19:20.420 --> 00:19:22.350
<i>If I break his serve, I'll win.</i>

00:19:22.350 --> 00:19:25.680
<i>Even if I can't do that, as long as 
I hold my next serve game, I'll win!</i>

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:27.240
<i>I can do this!</i>

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:37.690
But remember that in the end, the one who 
enjoys their time the most is the winner.

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:41.200
<i>Even if I'm winning, I can't let 
my guard down even for a second.</i>

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:43.450
<i>How am I supposed to enjoy a match like this?</i>

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:44.530
Time!

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:53.210
<i>I won't regret this!</i>

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:55.460
<i>I won't give up, either!</i>

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:56.960
15-love.

00:19:58.490 --> 00:19:59.460
15-all.

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:02.470
30-all.

00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:06.970
30-40.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:08.970
Maruo 

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:08.980
Bro's got match point!

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:34.080
Game, set, and match.

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:37.090
Winner: Maruo. Score: 6-2.

00:20:37.090 --> 00:20:38.340
Yes!

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:40.840
<i>I won...</i>

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:43.910
<i>I won with my skill!</i>

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:47.850
The third seed lost!

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:48.980
Are you serious?

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:50.930
Maruo's gotten so much stronger!

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:53.080
You did it!

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:55.830
Now you're in Kanto Junior, too, Bro!

00:20:55.830 --> 00:20:57.360
Yay!

00:20:55.830 --> 00:20:57.360
That's amazing, Ei-chan!

00:20:57.360 --> 00:20:59.010
Maruo won by a mile!

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:00.450
That's so unexpected!

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:02.360
Really?! Miyagawa-kun lost?

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:02.360
Thank you very much.

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:03.400
I can't believe it!

00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:04.620
No way!

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:07.620
I personally thought I played my best.

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:09.270
But you completely destroyed me.

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:11.590
You not only returned my drive volley,

00:21:11.590 --> 00:21:13.540
but also broke my serve twice.

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:15.340
Needless to say, I lost completely.

00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:18.380
Maruo-kun, you're better than I am.

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:19.910
Miyagawa-kun...

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:29.270
Oh, yeah. You asked me last summer 
if I was trying to go pro.

00:21:29.270 --> 00:21:29.840
Huh?

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:33.530
You're aiming to go pro too, right?

00:21:35.650 --> 00:21:36.310
Yes.

00:21:38.650 --> 00:21:41.420
Uh, are you looking to go pro, Miyagawa-kun?

00:21:41.420 --> 00:21:42.270
Go pro?

00:21:42.270 --> 00:21:44.150
Hmm, well...

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:45.840
Good question.

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:47.400
<i>He dodged it.</i>

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:51.300
<i>Maybe that's the difference between us.</i>

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:54.910
Good luck in the Kanto Junior.

00:21:56.540 --> 00:21:57.720
<i>Miyagawa-kun...</i>

00:21:58.330 --> 00:22:02.310
<i>Miyagawa-kun must've been playing 
with the same mindset as me.</i>

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:07.270
Hey.

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:10.710
I ended up losing.

00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:12.570
Don't worry about it!

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:13.970
It was a great game.

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:16.210
But...

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:18.980
I gave it my all.

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:20.980
Then,

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:22.890
there'll definitely be a next time.

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:24.660
Yeah.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:25.020
Next Time

00:24:00.750 --> 00:24:02.340
Unlike how it's been in the past,

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:04.380
it was a real struggle to win.

00:24:04.380 --> 00:24:07.250
I guess it showed me how 
intense a real match can be.

00:24:07.250 --> 00:24:09.590
I can understand how that feels.

00:24:09.590 --> 00:24:09.960
Huh?

00:24:10.430 --> 00:24:13.970
But you'll probably have more 
matches like that in the future.

00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:17.470
The more you win, the sooner you'll understand

00:24:17.470 --> 00:24:20.770
that the truly fun matches are 
the ones that feel that way.

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:22.770
Next time on <i>Baby Steps</i>,

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:25.020
Baffled by Intuition

00:24:22.770 --> 00:24:24.330
"Baffled by Intuition."

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:32.280
End
